e way colors shift between the input and output rings in the various \"trains,\" and the implications of the center color.\n\n\n**Refining Pattern Recognition**\n\nI've been re-examining the cyclic shift hypothesis and realized my previous assumptions were incomplete. Specifically, I've rechecked \"Train 3's\" input and output rings. I've corrected my ring index and color mapping assumptions, and now, I see it's a cyclic shift. The key is in understanding how the inner ring influences the outer ones. I am now double-checking the pattern with \"Train 3\".\n\n\n**Identifying Permutation Pattern**\n\nI've cracked it! I was missing a key insight. I see it now: it's a cyclic permutation of *unique* colors. I tested this against Train 3 and Train 1, and the mapping works perfectly. Every input color is replaced by its cyclically shifted equivalent, and the ring values correspond to the input. This appears to be the core principle!\n\n\n",
